WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.254 --> 00:00:02.670
JEFF DAVIS: Welcome to the
Google Cloud Platform education

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:03.210
series.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.240
My name is Jeff Davis,
and in this session

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.230
we'll discuss analyzing
your data in less time using

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.110
Google BigQuery.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.420
We'll be running a moderated
QA during the session,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.080
so feel free to use the
Ask your question field

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.270
at the bottom of your
screen to submit questions

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.380
as we go along.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.100
Let's get started.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.290
So the agenda for this
session will be starting out

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:28.880
with a series of demos.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.600
We'll show you typical
use cases and the speed

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.710
at which BigQuery operates
and how easy it is to use.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:39.740
Then we'll talk about the
general set of features

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.230
for BigQuery and how you
might be able to leverage them

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:43.620
in your environment.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.826
We'll talk about how to find
answers to questions after you

00:00:46.826 --> 00:00:48.200
leave today's
session and you get

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.910
started working with
BigQuery on your own.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.330
We'll talk about some best
practices for evaluating

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.190
BigQuery in your environment.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.390
And again, we'll be
doing Q&amp;A as we go along,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.000
so submit those questions.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.870
Let's start with
a series of demos

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.690
that show both BigQuery's
scale, as well as

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.480
how easy it is to
integrate in other systems

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.030
that you use for data
analysis and reporting.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:14.600
What we have here
is the web UI that

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.492
allows end users to easily
play with data within BigQuery.

00:01:18.492 --> 00:01:20.450
We're going to show you
some integrations later

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:25.220
on, but right now this is
just for doing quick iterative

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.900
analysis of large data sets.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.570
So let's start with
some sample data

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.970
sets that Google
makes available.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:36.650
So for instance, here we
have airline on time data.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.130
So we have a list of flights.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.630
And if we take a
look at this table,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.800
you can see it's
about an 8 GB file.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:48.470
It's 70 million rows of
data regarding different

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:53.000
airlines' on time, arrival,
and departure performance

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.740
in different states
and so forth.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.660
And I want to get
some information.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:04.560
So I'm going to start with a can
query that I've got here, OK?

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.310
And this is going to find
all the arrival entries just

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.000
for United Airlines.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.425
So I'm going to copy this,
go back, and query the table.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.540
OK, you can see I'm
using standard SQL.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.260
I'm going to be querying
the flights table,

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.460
looking for all rows where the
airline code is 19977, that's

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.080
the code for United Airlines.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.380
And I'm going to select
three columns from every row.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.920
Now I'm going to disable
caching because we

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.170
want to see how
long it takes to do

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.680
an initial query against
the 70 odd million rows.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.960
You'll notice it gives me
an estimate of how much data

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.210
it's going to have to process.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.200
This is also useful
for estimating

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.890
how much it's going to
cost and that you pay,

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:52.860
I think, it's $5 per
terabyte of data process

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.390
after the first terabyte.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.080
So this uses some
fraction of that.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.840
So I'm going to run
this and it should

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.725
take between 25 to 30 seconds
to complete the query.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:12.359
When it comes back, it
will show us the three

00:03:12.359 --> 00:03:13.400
rows for all the columns.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.650
It will give us a column count.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:19.460
And then we'll move on to a
slightly more interesting query

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.170
that gives us some more
insight into our data.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.800
So you'll notice it
took 23.8 seconds.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.650
Processed 2 gigabytes of data.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:37.610
It returned 5,043,578 rows.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.770
So it's a lot of data returned.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:41.930
That's part of why it
took so long to come back.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.240
It would have been
faster if there had been

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.880
fewer rows in the result set.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.880
So what this does is it just
gives us the date, the arrival

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.380
state, and the arrival delay.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.560
I would like to get a
little bit more insight,

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.390
so what I'm going to
do is run this query.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:02.930
This query should tell me
the average delay by month

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:07.370
by arrival state
for United Airlines.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.230
So I want to see what
months are the worst

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:11.450
and what states are the worst.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:12.440
So let's run this.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.660
Again, it's going to process
the same amount of data.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.250
That came back
quite a bit faster.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:24.070
And you'll notice that we
have 5,000 rows instead

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.080
of 5 million rows returned.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.319
That's part of why it
came back so quickly.

00:04:28.319 --> 00:04:29.860
And you'll notice
that what we've got

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:35.140
is for each state we've
got individual months.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:38.080
And for those months, we've got
the average delay, the number

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.199
of flights, the number of
flights that were early,

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:41.740
the number of flights
that were late,

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.490
the percentage of
flights that were early,

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:46.150
and the percentage of
flights that were late.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.345
So this is using the lingua
franca of data analysis.

00:04:49.345 --> 00:04:51.340
It's using standard SQL.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.560
It's not that
difficult to write.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:55.660
There's no actual query
optimization here.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.500
You'll notice I'm
also using subqueries.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.120
That's perfectly valid.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.720
And I was able to
very quickly get back

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.772
insights from a very
large collection of data.

00:05:08.772 --> 00:05:11.230
So again, you can do this all
interactively through the web

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:11.990
UI.

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:14.860
Let's actually
look at just scale.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:18.160
How fast can this go on
really big data sets?

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.762
So you think, well,
Jeff, 75 million rows.

00:05:21.762 --> 00:05:22.970
That's a pretty big data set.

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:27.390
Well, it's not as big as some of
these data sets, for instance.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:32.150
Here we have Wikipedia
benchmark data.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:35.180
And if you look, you can
see that the table basically

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.240
has got year, month, day,
project, language, title,

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:38.740
and view.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:41.350
So this has got data
about how many times

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.990
particular articles were viewed.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:47.050
So we've got a
million row version.

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:50.260
We have a 10 million
row version, 100 million

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:53.230
row version, a
billion row version.

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:55.300
Let's see how this all scales.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:59.290
So we'll start with a million.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.530
I'm going to turn off the
standard SQL and use legacy

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:06.200
SQL just for giggles.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:09.280
So we're going to query
a million articles.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:12.640
We're looking for all
articles that have

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:15.169
Davis because I'm fairly vain.

00:06:15.169 --> 00:06:16.960
I'm going to look for
articles about people

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.880
with my same last name.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.000
We're going to use a regex
expression to match these,

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.240
and what I want to do is
actually group them by title

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.710
and sum the number of views.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.860
So let's do this over
a million row data set.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:33.140
This is 47 megabytes.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.330
And it should come
back fairly quickly.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:38.020
So you'll notice Anthony
Davis and Thomas Davis

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:42.660
are the most frequently viewed
articles in the 1 million

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:43.610
entry data set.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.470
Let's look at 10 million.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:53.830
OK, so we go from 47
megabytes to 476 megabytes.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:07:01.630
And we go from just a couple
of seconds to three seconds.

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:05.650
We now have roughly
2,500 results.

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:08.830
Daniel Davis is the top now.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.300
So this was three
seconds to do 10 million.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.134
Let's do 100 million.

00:07:13.134 --> 00:07:14.800
And you see where
we're going with this.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.350
You want to know if I'm going to
get to the 100 billion number,

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:17.910
and I am.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.920
I'm going to have to pay $20 out
of my pocket to run that query,

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.850
but I'll do it just for you.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:25.300
So you notice, ah,
Jefferson Davis.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:26.530
Not my namesake.

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:28.060
It's a coincidence.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.880
Favorite jazz musician,
Miles Davis, is up there.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.710
So we've done 100 million.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:35.440
Let's do, oh, what the heck.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:36.400
That took two seconds.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.390
Let's do 100 billion.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:43.020
This is going to process
3.64 terabytes of data,

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:45.105
100 billion rows.

00:07:45.105 --> 00:07:45.855
Cross our fingers.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:51.740
Get out my ATM card.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:53.000
And yet, think about it.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:56.090
Twenty dollars to run this
query versus the amortized cost

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:58.160
of a query against a
big Teradata system,

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:01.770
or some other big data
rig that you've got.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.800
It's going to give you
equivalent performance.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.760
It's actually a heck of a value.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:15.975
So I think it's about 30 to 50
seconds to complete the query.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.170
While that's running, I'm going
to get-- oh, actually it's

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:23.670
done.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.910
So 37 seconds.

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:31.890
Notice it came back with
185,000 rows in the results

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.789
and it has aggregated and told
me that Miles Davis is in fact

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:40.980
the most popular article within
the 100 billion row data set.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:41.924
So think about it.

00:08:41.924 --> 00:08:45.210
That's 30 seconds to do
analysis on 100 billion rows.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:48.750
I could sit here and
iterate on my queries,

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:55.590
interact almost in real time
with my data very, very easily.

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:59.100
Now obviously, doing this
interactively with the web UI

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:00.370
is one thing.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:03.120
Another thing is to
actually expose this data,

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:05.460
pull it out of
BigQuery and expose it

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:06.870
through some other application.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.580
So we have an example here.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:12.180
This is a customer
success application

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:15.120
that pulls data from BigQuery.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.630
So let's take a look at
one of the dashboards.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:22.870
If you went to the
App Engine session,

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.370
you saw this dashboard earlier.

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:29.440
This has basically got a
bunch of visualizations

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:33.250
of different types of
user engagement metrics

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:35.120
within my product.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:38.650
And what this allows me to do
is pull the data out of BigQuery

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:41.800
through APIs and
represent it in code.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:46.790
So behind the scenes here
I've got a user events table.

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:51.850
Now, this table is
paltry 500,000 rows,

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:56.350
but it's a modest amount of data
for the usage of this product.

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:58.360
And it's a demo
application, so it doesn't

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:01.810
have tens of millions of rows.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:05.050
What we do then is we
write a little bit of code

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:06.640
to access this.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:10.000
And here's the code.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.000
We're going to go to BigQuery.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.500
This is a little Python script.

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:17.980
You'll notice what we do
is we import a library.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.520
So Google has code libraries
or client libraries

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:24.610
available for a variety
of different languages,

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:26.120
and it's quite simple.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:31.990
We create a BigQuery client,
we connect to the data set,

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:36.190
and then within the data
set, we connect to a table.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:41.320
And within the table, we are
then going to run a query.

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:46.300
If we want to run an
arbitrary query for instance,

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:50.520
we take our client, we run a
query, we designate the query.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.860
We give it a time out,
so this makes sure

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:56.190
that the query has up
to 45 seconds to run.

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:58.264
We run the query,
and then we simply

00:10:58.264 --> 00:10:59.430
iterate through the results.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.160
And this allows us to
process through the results

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:03.460
of the query.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:05.220
If you want to
write to BigQuery,

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:06.370
it's quite straightforward.

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:10.530
You simply take the table
and you insert a row.

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:13.320
And this adds events
into the backend.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:16.800
So this data gets written
automatically, then the API

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:19.890
pulls it out and
renders it in dashboards

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:21.193
within your application.

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:30.117
Now you may be using a different
tool to do your visualization

00:11:30.117 --> 00:11:30.700
and reporting.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:32.716
You may not use the
web UI and you may not

00:11:32.716 --> 00:11:34.840
want to go so far as to
write a custom dashboarding

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:35.450
application.

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:37.120
Let's say you want
to use something

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:40.960
like Tableau or Looker, or in
this case, I'm using a product

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:43.210
called Mode Analytics.

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:46.930
These BI visualization
and analysis tools

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:50.196
allow you to connect to
BigQuery as a backend.

00:11:50.196 --> 00:11:51.320
So you'll notice over here.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:53.080
Let me make this a
little bit bigger.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:54.920
You'll notice I
have data sources.

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:58.510
If I click on the plus
sign, I can actually

00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:01.030
select a backend
data source, and I

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:04.260
want to connect to BigQuery.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:05.590
OK.

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:07.510
So it's saying I want
to connect to BigQuery.

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:10.010
It wants to know my project ID.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:15.250
So I am going to pick my project
ID, which is GCP webinar demos.

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:23.020
And then I need to
create a service account.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:27.010
And so the service
account is created

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:32.850
through IAM, the identity
and access management.

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:36.660
And you'll notice that I've got
a variety of service accounts

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:37.350
here.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:41.980
I can go ahead and create
a new service account.

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:45.580
Actually, sorry, service
accounts are here.

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:47.080
Those were the roles.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:49.760
I'm going to create
a new service account

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.300
and I'm going to call this mode.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:56.950
And I want to give this
service account BigQuery admin

00:12:56.950 --> 00:13:00.160
permissions, so that it can
create tables, delete tables,

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:01.880
read data, and so forth.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:04.250
And I want to get
a new private key.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.460
And because I read the
Mode documentation,

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:08.710
I know that it needs
to be a P12 key.

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:11.760
So I'm going to create
this service account.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:13.430
And I get the credentials.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:16.640
OK?

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:25.185
Actually we'll
cancel out of that.

00:13:25.185 --> 00:13:26.810
I don't want to put
it in my key chain.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.340
We'll just skip over that part.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:31.137
We go back to Mode and
we've got to give it

00:13:31.137 --> 00:13:32.470
the name of the service account.

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:34.761
So the name of the service
account or the email address

00:13:34.761 --> 00:13:36.740
here is this.

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:41.960
So we designate that and then
we have to upload the key file.

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:46.150
And that should be the key file.

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:57.220
And if all went well, this
has now connected my tool

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:59.440
with BigQuery.

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:04.080
So I can create a new query.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:10.050
Let's pick one that looks a lot
like what I was using before.

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:11.610
Let's not use the 100 billion.

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:15.900
We can use the 100 million.

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:18.100
OK, now I need to enter a query.

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:22.260
So I'm going to go pick one
of my pre-canned queries.

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.050
Let's go ahead and use
the standard SQL format

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:29.610
for getting information about
United's on time arrival.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:30.840
I can paste that in.

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:35.605
It will run the query.

00:14:35.605 --> 00:14:39.610
It gives me a tabular
version of the results.

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:43.810
And if I want to, I can
create a chart for instance

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:46.660
that shows on time arrival.

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:50.980
I can change the chart
type if I want to.

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:52.160
This sort of thing.

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:56.890
So this shows how you can do
analysis and visualization

00:14:56.890 --> 00:15:00.550
within a third party tool,
so you can fit BigQuery

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:06.220
into your normal data analysis
and analytics workflows.

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:07.550
Pretty cool.

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:08.360
OK.

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:13.780
Let's switch back and talk
about BigQuery's functionality

00:15:13.780 --> 00:15:14.470
overall.

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:15.880
We showed you some pieces of it.

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:19.000
Let's highlight
additional capabilities

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:22.030
and use cases so
that you'll have

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:26.110
a better understanding of how to
apply it in your environments.

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:29.590
So again, BigQuery is
this fully managed,

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:33.100
massive scale, low
cost enterprise data

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:35.200
warehouse that Google
provides running

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:40.200
on top of their compute storage
and networking infrastructure.

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:45.190
There is a demo query that
I've seen at some conferences

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:51.700
that when you run it, it takes
3,300 cores, 330 hard drives,

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:56.770
and 330 gigabits of network
bandwidth for 30 seconds

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:58.680
to complete.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:01.110
Now those are
tremendous numbers.

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:05.820
But because Google has so
much available capacity,

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:09.540
they're able to give you a slice
of 30 seconds with that level

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:12.240
of resources to run
your query and then

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.250
claw those resources
back and deploy them

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:16.220
against somebody else's query.

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:18.799
There is no infrastructure
for you to manage.

00:16:18.799 --> 00:16:21.090
You'll often hear this referred
to as being serverless.

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:23.112
Well, it's clearly
not serverless

00:16:23.112 --> 00:16:24.570
because there are
servers involved,

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:27.240
but you don't have to
deal with any servers.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:29.340
You focus just on
getting your data

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.934
in and on analyzing your data.

00:16:31.934 --> 00:16:34.350
You don't have to worry about
standing up servers, tearing

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:37.220
down servers, configuration
updates, any of that stuff.

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:43.260
So when you perform
queries, you're

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:45.820
going to get results
really, really quick.

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:52.110
So we showed scanning terabytes
of data in roughly 35 seconds.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:55.180
At the petabyte level,
you're talking a few minutes.

00:16:55.180 --> 00:16:58.900
So this actually makes turning
around analysis on a huge data

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:00.730
sets very, very fast.

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:04.510
Which means better
analysis, more creativity,

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:07.720
more interesting insights
that you can derive.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:09.520
It uses standard SQL.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:13.260
I showed you that there
is a legacy SQL syntax,

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:16.270
but in the newest generation
of their query language

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:20.530
it's ANSII 2011 SQL
compliant, so you can actually

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:23.230
lift queries from
other environments

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:25.750
and with very little
tweaking if any they will run

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:28.099
in the BigQuery environment.

00:17:28.099 --> 00:17:31.100
You can use the web
UI as I demonstrated,

00:17:31.100 --> 00:17:33.590
or you can use third
party tools of choice,

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:36.360
like Looker, Tableau, Click.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:38.260
They've got date
partition tables,

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:41.520
so you can actually have
BigQuery automatically

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:46.170
partition time series
data by day or by date.

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:49.750
And this allows you to have
much higher performance queries.

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:53.710
You can also have BigQuery query
against external data sources.

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:56.520
So you can have a bunch of
data inside of BigQuery,

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:59.790
and then you can join that data
against external source data

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:01.830
as well.

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:07.650
How do you get these massive
amounts of data into BigQuery?

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:09.450
Well, you can do
this interactively.

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:12.960
Like you can upload
files through the web UI,

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:16.140
or you can use the BQ
command line utility

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:19.760
and upload a file
from your local PC.

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:21.720
However, obviously that's
going to work better

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:23.760
for a few megabytes
of data than it will

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:25.900
for a few terabytes of data.

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:28.200
So there are other ways
that make more sense

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.450
for transferring
really large volumes.

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:33.660
You can stream data
directly into BigQuery

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:36.250
query at 100,000
rows per second.

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:39.810
So one of the things
that we did at Highfive,

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:42.750
this video conferencing
startup that I used to work at,

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:44.910
is we actually would generate--

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:47.340
So you had 10,000
users and calls.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.730
For all 10,000 calls
we were generating

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:54.840
second by second
telemetry data on hundreds

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:57.690
of different counters
on every single call.

00:18:57.690 --> 00:18:59.850
We were writing that into
a production database,

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:02.490
but then streaming
it into BigQuery.

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:04.740
And BigQuery was the
data store from which

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:06.990
the customer facing
team dashboards

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:10.220
drew all of their data.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:15.080
Google Cloud Dataflow is
a data pipeline framework

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:20.030
that allows you to easily stand
up ETL sort of transformation

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:23.870
jobs that take data
from incoming sources,

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:26.000
manipulate the data,
and write them out

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:27.500
into some target repository.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:30.830
Dataflow works
beautifully with BigQuery.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:33.500
And so you can easily
be processing batch data

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:37.010
or streaming data and pumping it
through a transformation engine

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:38.870
into BigQuery.

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:42.200
There's also the newly announced
BigQuery Transfer Service

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:47.900
where you can set up regular
transfers of your YouTube data,

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:51.260
your DoubleClick, data
and your AdWords data

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:53.900
and have that stream
automatically into BigQuery

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:55.130
for performing analytics.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:56.780
There will be
additional services

00:19:56.780 --> 00:20:00.320
coming to BigQuery Transfer
Service in the future.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:04.850
Right now through middle of
2017 BigQuery Transfer Service

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:05.840
is free.

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:09.590
After the midpoint
in the year, it

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:13.670
will become a billable
monthly service.

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:14.900
Availability and access.

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:19.310
Your data is replicated
across multiple geographies.

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:23.120
What that means is that the
durability and availability are

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:24.320
through the roof.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:26.991
If you have a device go down,
if you have a zone go down,

00:20:26.991 --> 00:20:28.490
if you have a region
go down, you're

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:31.760
still going to be able to
get access to your data,

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:34.310
and you will not lose any data.

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:38.910
You also then can control
who can access that data.

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:43.107
So there are identity and
access management roles

00:20:43.107 --> 00:20:43.940
that you can assign.

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:47.780
So you can say developers
can write data,

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:50.690
and your analysts can read data.

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:53.150
Or your end users can read data.

00:20:53.150 --> 00:20:58.220
You also can set permissions
at the per data set level.

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:03.190
You have really high
availability, as I mentioned.

00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:06.380
A 99.9% SLA on availability.

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:11.480
The data that you store in
BigQuery is encrypted at rest.

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:14.300
We mentioned the fact that
you can use IAM roles,

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:18.500
and the fact that you can either
go through command line or web

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:21.990
UI or external clients.

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:22.950
Pricing.

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:27.990
So there's two components
to pricing for BigQuery.

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:32.340
There's a storage component and
a query processing component.

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:34.140
So the storage
component, you pay

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:37.300
$0.02 per gigabyte per month.

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:41.560
If you store the data
for longer than 30 days,

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:43.880
you actually get a
discount on that.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:45.820
So if the data
doesn't change but you

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:50.710
keep querying against it, the
price automatically goes down.

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:56.500
You pay $5 per terabyte of
data processed for queries.

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:59.200
The first terabyte is free.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:03.340
For companies that do
more than $10,000 a month

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:07.610
in analytics processing
on a monthly basis,

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:10.360
they can actually get
flat rate pricing.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.220
And you buy what
are called slots.

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:15.820
It's sort of technical,
but it's a different model

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:19.210
for processing fees.

00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:22.540
And you can get what
essentially is a discount

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:25.210
by using flat rate pricing.

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:27.400
If your volume goes
above $10,000 a month,

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:29.800
certainly up to about
$40,000, $50,000 a month,

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:33.970
talk to your Google sales
rep and they can hook you up.

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:36.640
It should be noted
that BigQuery is not

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:42.550
a replacement for every
enterprise data store.

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:45.650
It's not a transactional
relational database.

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:50.320
So if you're looking to read
and write into individual cells

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:53.624
in rows in a table and
constantly update that,

00:22:53.624 --> 00:22:54.790
that's not what this is for.

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:59.980
BigQuery is data that you write
in, you don't change the data,

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.510
but you're doing reporting
against the data.

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:05.560
Similarly, it's not an
operational data store,

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:08.000
and it is a
cloud-based solution.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:10.030
So if you have an
application that

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:14.620
requires on premise
data warehousing,

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:17.570
BigQuery would
not work for that.

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:21.350
But you want to ask
yourself, are the benefits

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:24.740
of BigQuery worth
making the changes

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:28.340
necessary in our workflow to
put this data in the cloud?

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:30.260
A lot of organizations
didn't think

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.462
that they would want
to or be able to do

00:23:33.462 --> 00:23:35.420
their data warehousing
in the cloud are finding

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:37.790
that the benefits are great
enough that they can find

00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:41.010
creative ways to make it work.

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:43.970
So given this, you want to
work with your internal teams

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:48.150
to figure out what can
we do with BigQuery.

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:52.010
So what Shine
technologies did was

00:23:52.010 --> 00:23:53.510
they were working
with a customer

00:23:53.510 --> 00:23:57.230
that did a huge amount
of internet advertising.

00:23:57.230 --> 00:23:59.690
And what this
customer wanted to be

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:02.180
able to present to
their executives

00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:07.670
was a live dashboard that showed
the performance of their ads.

00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:11.540
So what they needed to have was
the ability to stream millions

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:16.430
and millions of ad
impressions into BigQuery,

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:20.730
generate the analytics on
this constantly updated data

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:24.320
set that had billions
of rows, and do

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:27.350
complex queries to
drive insights, and then

00:24:27.350 --> 00:24:28.970
visualize these.

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:33.500
And Shine was able to do this
without any Hadoop clusters,

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:38.300
without any sophisticated
systems operations investment.

00:24:38.300 --> 00:24:41.840
And they were able to deliver
the clients' dashboards

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:46.040
that would consistently
in 20 to 30 seconds

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:49.740
render visualizations
of up to date data.

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:50.790
Saved tons of money.

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:52.260
Actually became a
product that they

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:57.627
were able to start delivering
to other customers as well.

00:24:57.627 --> 00:24:59.460
Now when you walk away
from today's session,

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:01.620
you may have queries as
you're trying things out

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:04.530
and you may need guidance on
how to perform certain tasks,

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:07.830
so where do you look
for data or information?

00:25:07.830 --> 00:25:10.150
How do you find what
you're looking for?

00:25:10.150 --> 00:25:13.440
So as always, you want to start
with search, and just type

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:15.366
in BigQuery and your topic.

00:25:15.366 --> 00:25:16.740
So for instance,
you want to know

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:18.810
how to get data into BigQuery.

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:20.340
BigQuery ingestion.

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:23.220
And you'll notice you've got
preparing data for loading,

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:27.060
streaming data in, improving
data ingestion times,

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:28.962
and so forth, OK?

00:25:28.962 --> 00:25:30.420
So for instance,
this article talks

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:34.230
about using Avro, which
compresses the data before it

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:36.660
transmits it in and decompresses
it on the other end.

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:38.280
This is built into
BigQuery query.

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:42.650
It makes data loading
much more efficient.

00:25:42.650 --> 00:25:46.060
There's also the typical
table of contents

00:25:46.060 --> 00:25:47.980
in BigQuery documentation.

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:53.130
So let me actually take
you on a quick tour.

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:56.305
So if we type in BigQuery.

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:04.060
Up here at the top will be
the splash page from Google.

00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:07.120
If you click on
View BigQuery docs,

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:09.460
you'll get the typical
table of contents.

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:11.720
There's a Quickstart here.

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:12.350
Actually two.

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:14.391
One for the web UI, and
one for the command line.

00:26:14.391 --> 00:26:16.940
These get you up and running
in less than five minutes.

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:19.340
They give you a
very quick taste.

00:26:19.340 --> 00:26:22.120
And then you've got a drill
down into how to guide.

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:23.990
So you can see how
do I load data?

00:26:23.990 --> 00:26:26.150
How do I prepare the
data before I load it?

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:28.700
How do I transform the data?

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:34.160
How do I load it from data
store, or cloud storage,

00:26:34.160 --> 00:26:35.930
or via API?

00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:36.860
How do I stream it in?

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:39.470
And then here's,
how do I do queries?

00:26:39.470 --> 00:26:42.980
And there's a section down
here on how do I export data?

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:46.370
And then general basics around
security, and management,

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:47.330
and so forth.

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:51.890
So pretty well organized,
reasonably exhaustive

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:52.790
reference.

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:54.960
Should be very useful to you.

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:57.260
Now, I don't have a
prescribed methodology

00:26:57.260 --> 00:27:00.920
for how to do an
evaluation of BigQuery

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:04.370
because it really depends
on your applications.

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:09.260
I've heard two very different
approaches commonly used

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:13.430
by companies that have large
scale data warehousing needs.

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:17.900
One is to take your
top half dozen queries.

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:21.260
So your top half
dozen jobs that are

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:24.050
breaking on your
existing infrastructure,

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:28.310
or just take a long time,
and move them to BigQuery.

00:27:28.310 --> 00:27:31.980
So you just pick five or six
queries, you load your data,

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:34.730
you run the queries, you see
how much better they are.

00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:36.980
If they're better, great.

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:38.720
You're ready to go.

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:43.490
On the other end of the spectrum
are companies where they really

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:47.930
are going to be very methodical
and exhaustive about the way

00:27:47.930 --> 00:27:49.170
they do the evaluation.

00:27:49.170 --> 00:27:53.490
So before they can move any
of their data into the cloud,

00:27:53.490 --> 00:27:57.290
they have to do compliance
and regulatory research.

00:27:57.290 --> 00:27:59.360
They have to cleanse
all of their data.

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:01.250
They may need to
anonymize the data.

00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:03.620
They load massive
amounts of data

00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:05.690
and then they
essentially replicate

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:10.100
their existing analytics
workflows in BigQuery.

00:28:10.100 --> 00:28:13.460
And then they may go so
far as to actually do

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:15.320
blind taste tests.

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:18.270
So they have their front end
representation be identical,

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:21.490
but they have half of the users
go through the new BigQuery

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:24.650
enabled pipeline, and half
the users go through the old.

00:28:24.650 --> 00:28:28.699
And they judge the efficacy,
the level of satisfaction,

00:28:28.699 --> 00:28:29.240
and so forth.

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:30.945
So it's going to be
up to you, and it

00:28:30.945 --> 00:28:32.570
will depend on what
vertical you're in,

00:28:32.570 --> 00:28:34.790
what data you're looking
at, and so forth.

00:28:34.790 --> 00:28:36.680
Certainly, quick
and dirty way to get

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:39.770
a sense of what the
potential benefits are

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:42.320
is to do the half dozen queries.

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:45.680
And then depending on your
long-term applications,

00:28:45.680 --> 00:28:49.640
you may need to do a much more
exhaustive proof of concept.

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:51.440
So that's it for this session.

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:52.920
Thanks so much for joining us.

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:56.860
I hope you found it
interesting and helpful.

00:28:56.860 --> 00:28:58.610
We're going to send
you a follow-up email,

00:28:58.610 --> 00:29:00.193
and it will have a
link to a code lab,

00:29:00.193 --> 00:29:03.500
so you'll get a guided walk
through BigQuery that you

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:05.030
can do on your own.

00:29:05.030 --> 00:29:06.530
We'd love to get
your feedback, so

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:09.410
feel free to provide
the feedback in the UI

00:29:09.410 --> 00:29:12.580
in front of you, and please
stay tuned for the next session.

